Klaus Kassner wrote:

> [...] So I knew that Vecna is tough.  On the other
> hand, taking him on is attractive, because he will leave a hand
> that gives half physical damage if carried and level drain
> resistance if wielded (only useful for retreating, I suppose).

Level drain resistance is mandatory in Slash'em.  At this stage of the
game I normally wear an amulet of drain resistance or deep dragon
scales.

[...]
> I gave up on the lycanthrope, when his max hitpoints were down
> to 1 due to excessively having been killed in polymorphed form...
> Probably I will have to play quite a number of games before
> being able to ascend a lycanthrope.

Lycanthropes are extremly difficult or, at least for me, extremely
annoying to play.  Only when you manage to get to the higher experience
levels will you have enough power points to return to human form.  Once
while playing a lycanthropic barbarian I got an early wish from a pool
on dungeon level one, and I wished for an amulet of unchanging; only
then was I able to bring this character quite far.  Furthermore,
lycanthropes have to eat like combine harvesters due to their intrinsic
regeneration (which sometimes is a life-saver, unless you don't find
enough food or get enough piety for the next food prayer).

> [...] I was consternated on the one hand and
> hilarious on the other, telling myself that from now on, I
> would be much more careful about cockatrices...

Heh, you just plainly were not cautious.  Nearly all roguelikes are
paradigms for games of alertness, cautiosness and patience.  NetHack
(and its variants) and maybe even more so ADOM are good examples.  I'll
never forget how my character turned to stone while trying to help a
cockatrice out of a pit (such a poor sod!).

[...]
> Well, I went there without my pets (elementals, cats, dogs),
> in order to be able to carry a ring of conflict to have fewer
> problems with monsters summoned by Vecna.

Normally, rings of conflict may be considered as wimpy, but they are,
Imho, allowed against heavy duty summoning monsters.  Playing the Lethe
patched NetHack without a ring of conflict would actually mean to play
an extinctionalist game.

> [Vecna, "You feel deathly cold."]

The last time I challenged Vecna was in an older Slash'em version,
maybe one year or so ago.  Then Vecna did only magic damage ("Your head
suddenly aches!" or similar) of about 30-50 HP, and cast some nasty
spells (like the always funny pool spells).  Facing Vecna without magic
resistance might be suicide.

> Since I was neutral, I did not want to kill One-eyed Sam,
> although I had figured out how to do that safely in one of the
> explore mode games.  I just collected all the stuff I wanted,
> including two magic lamps, went to the top left corner of the
> store, polymorphed into a xorn and left the shop without paying.

Whoah, polymorphing is for wimps!  But seriously, Ony-eyed Sam is the
deadliest opponent you can fight against in Slash'em; don't even think
to make him (or her?) angry without having two or more blessed potions
of invulnerability, a blessed +6/+7 weapon and drain resistance.

> [...] One of the wishes from the four magic lamps
> he owned till now went for a blessed figurine of a planetar.
> (Would a solar work, too? My strongest pets so far have been
> planetars.)

Of course!  Solar pets are fun!  Give them some nice equipment to pump
up their AC so they will be much less susceptible to the swallowing
attacks of purple worms and trappers.  You could also give them an
amulet of life saving, but they won't need it anyways.  Make them
invisible and fast (throw a potion) if they don't do it themselves.
And give'em a nice shiny +7 lawful artifact weapon like Sunsword or
Demonbane (use a whip to yank their weapon from their hands).  Having
two or three Solars stomping around the dungeon, killing everything for
you (save cockatrices and their ilk) is like having a private army.
Planetars are virtually like Solars, only with less hit points and
lower maximum experience level.  Both can kill the Wizard of Yendor in
several turns.

> [killing Vecna and Demogorgon with bullets]

Nice, never did it that way.  I never even tried to use firearms
because they and the bullets are just too damn heavy.

> Now I have to watch out for those diamond and sapphire golems
> mostly [...]

And steel golems; use them if you have a +6 weapon to bring them to +5
so you have another try for this extra little +7.  And if you fight
them bare-handed (tough guy!), beware: your gauntlets might be
affected, too.

Have fun!


